<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Purépecha Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1530 CE (approx.) · capital Tzintzuntzan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second largest state of Mesoamerica at the Spanish arrival, centred on Lake Pátzcuaro in Michoacán, which defeated the Aztecs and worked copper and bronze.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Purépecha (Tarascans) developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a> basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a>, the leader Tariacuri united the lake communities in the 14th century, and his heirs divided the realm among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patzcuaro">Pátzcuaro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ihuatzio">Ihuatzio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a>, later reunited under Tzintzuntzan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Purépecha state was highly centralised under a king (cazonci) who ruled through officials and governors, and it worshipped the fire god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri">Curicaueri</a>. Its people, speaking a language unrelated to others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, were skilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze metallurgy, featherwork and lacquer. Its capital had distinctive keyhole-shaped yácata temples. In 1478-79 it routed an Aztec army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangaxuan-ii">Tangaxuan II</a>, submitted to the Spanish peacefully in 1522 but was burnt to death by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a> in 1530. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a> later organised the Purépecha communities, and their language is still spoken.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/purepecha-empire.jpg" alt="Purépecha Empire" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: Maunus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarascan_aztec_states.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1530 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tzintzuntzan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tariacuri, Tzitzipandacuare, Tangaxuan II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Purépecha religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Purépecha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mexico</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tarascan state, Tarascan Empire, Irechecua Tzintzuntzan</dd></dl></aside></div>
